  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Project for the Improvement of Camps for Displaced Persons from Myanmar and Host Communities in Cox&#x2019;s Bazar District</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over one million Rohingyas who fled from systematic persecution, discrimination, and other human rights violations in Rakhine State, Myanmar to Cox&apos;s Bazar, Bangladesh currently live in 33 congested camps formally designated by the Government of Bangladesh in Ukhiya and Teknaf Upazilas of Cox&apos;s Bazar District. The host communities also continue to confront issues that contribute to their vulnerabilities related to strained resources, disaster shocks, and the environment. Relationships between displaced persons and host communities in Cox&apos;s Bazar vary significantly and are highly dependent on the history and stability of the camps and camp-like settlements. As the situation protracts, however, relations within the different displaced person groupings and between displaced persons and Bangladeshi host communities are expected to increasingly give rise to shifting power dynamics, extortive practices, and disputes over access to land, resources, and livelihoods.Building upon the success of past programming for the displaced persons from Myanmar and host communities, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is supporting vulnerable communities through specialized water, sanitation and hygiene, disaster risk reduction, shelter, site management, and site development and protection services in camps and host communities.</narrative>
  </description>
